# Well-Architected

A digest of the AWS Well-Architected Framework and Amazon ECS's best
practices, for this repository: one section per pillar, what the pillar asks
of a container service on Fargate behind a load balancer, the CDK Nag rules (`AwsSolutions` pack) that enforce it when
`cdk synth` runs, and what you do by hand where no rule does. Sources and the
date they were read are in `docs/style/SOURCES.md`. This file belongs to the
Standard: a task never changes it.

`make check` runs CDK Nag's `AwsSolutions` checks on every stack
(`infra/standard.ts`): a finding fails synth unless it is acknowledged, on
one construct, with a reason. `infra/standard.test.ts` checks what CDK Nag
doesn't: tags, wildcard actions, encryption, log retention, removal policies.

## Operational excellence

_Run and monitor the system, and improve it in small, reversible changes._

- Everything is code: the stacks in `infra/` are the only way resources are
  made. No change by hand in the console.
- Changes are small, frequent and checked: `make check` synthesizes and
  lints every change; nothing here deploys.
- Observe: structured JSON logs with the request id, a log group per
  container, the load balancer's access logs and the VPC's flow log, each
  kept for a set time, and Container Insights on the cluster.
  - `AwsSolutions-ECS7`: every container logs.
  - `AwsSolutions-ECS4`: the cluster has Container Insights.
  - `AwsSolutions-ELB2`: the load balancer has access logs.
  - `AwsSolutions-VPC7`: the VPC has a flow log.
  - `infra/standard.test.ts` checks that every log group has a retention.
- Deploy safely: a deployment starts new tasks before stopping old ones
  (minimum healthy 100 %), and the circuit breaker rolls it back when the new
  tasks never pass the load balancer's health check.
- Trace when the issue asks for it: X-Ray tracing (an AWS Distro for
  OpenTelemetry collector beside the app in the task)
  costs per trace and needs `xray:Put*` on `*`, which CDK Nag flags
  (`AwsSolutions-IAM5`); acknowledge that finding with the reason.
- Tag everything so cost and ownership are traceable: every stack carries
  `c4factory:project`, `c4factory:system` and `c4factory:container`
  (`infra/standard.ts`, tested).
- By hand: alarms on errors, 5xx answers, unhealthy targets and CPU, and a runbook, when the
  system's needs ask for them.

## Security

_Protect data, systems and assets: identity, least privilege, encryption,
traceability, and protection at every layer._

- Least privilege: the task role, which the code runs as, is granted
  through CDK grants, such as
  `table.grant(taskDefinition.taskRole, 'dynamodb:GetItem')`, only the
  calls the code makes; the execution role,
  which ECS uses to pull the image and write logs, only what CDK grants it.
  - `AwsSolutions-IAM4`: no AWS managed policies on a role.
  - `AwsSolutions-IAM5`: no wildcard actions or resources without an
    acknowledgement naming the one finding and why.
  - `infra/standard.test.ts`: no `*` or `service:*` action anywhere.
- Protect the network: the tasks run in private subnets without public IPs
  and take traffic only from the load balancer's security group; only the
  load balancer faces the internet.
  - `AwsSolutions-EC23`: a security group open to `0.0.0.0/0`; the load
    balancer's is, as a public service's must be, and is acknowledged with
    that reason. No other group may be.
  - `AwsSolutions-VPC3`: network ACLs are used sparingly; security groups
    do the work.
- Authenticate requests where the issue asks for it: the load balancer can
  authenticate users (an OIDC provider or Cognito) on its HTTPS listener, or
  the service checks a token itself. A route without authentication is a
  choice the issue states.
- Encrypt at rest and in transit.
  - Clients speak HTTPS to the load balancer, with TLS 1.3 and 1.2 only
    (`SslPolicy.RECOMMENDED_TLS`); port 80 only redirects. The load balancer
    drops invalid header fields.
  - `AwsSolutions-S10`, `AwsSolutions-SQS4`, `AwsSolutions-SNS3`: requests
    must use TLS.
  - `AwsSolutions-SQS2`, `AwsSolutions-EFS1`: encryption at rest; DynamoDB
    is always encrypted, and `infra/standard.test.ts` requires a KMS key
    (`SSESpecification`) on every table, encryption on every bucket, queue
    and topic.
  - `AwsSolutions-KMS5`: customer-managed keys rotate.
- Protect data from the public: `AwsSolutions-S2` (bucket public access
  blocked), `AwsSolutions-S5`, `AwsSolutions-EVB1` (no open event bus
  policy), `AwsSolutions-ECR1` (no open image repository).
  A WAF web ACL on the load balancer is for the issue that asks for it.
- Secrets live in Secrets Manager, rotated (`AwsSolutions-SMG4`), read at
  start-up or passed as ECS `secrets`; never in an environment variable, the template, a log or the
  repository.
  `AwsSolutions-ECS2` flags every plain environment variable: acknowledge
  it only when each one is plainly not a secret (a table's name), and say so.
- The container runs as an unprivileged user on a read-only root file
  system; the image holds the bundle and Node, nothing else.
- By hand: validate all input at the boundary; never log user data or
  tokens; never return internal errors to a client.

## Reliability

_Recover from failure, scale with demand, and keep working when a part
fails._

- Multiple Availability Zones: the VPC spans two, the load balancer serves
  from both and the service keeps at least two tasks, which ECS spreads over
  them; DynamoDB spreads on its own. The one NAT gateway is in one zone: it
  carries only image pulls and logs, and a second costs as much again.
- Health checks: the load balancer checks `/health` and replaces a task that
  fails; the check reads nothing, so a DynamoDB outage doesn't stop every
  task.
- Scale with demand: the service scales on CPU between its bounds.
- Back up data and recover it: point-in-time recovery on every table, and
  data that outlives its stack.
  - `AwsSolutions-DDB3`: point-in-time recovery.
  - `AwsSolutions-RDS10`: deletion protection on databases; set it on tables
    too (`deletionProtection: true`).
  - `infra/standard.test.ts`: every stateful resource states its removal
    policy; data is `RETAIN`.
- Handle failures in asynchronous work: a queue or event source has a
  dead-letter queue.
  - `AwsSolutions-SQS3`: a queue has a DLQ or is one.
- Idempotency: a retried request changes nothing twice (whole-item puts,
  conditional writes, idempotency keys).
- Limits and shutdown: a body limit on every request; keep-alive longer than
  the load balancer's 60-second idle timeout; on `SIGTERM` the server stops
  taking requests and finishes those in flight, within the container's stop
  timeout; ECS sends it only after the load balancer has drained the task.

## Performance efficiency

_Use resources efficiently as demand changes and technology evolves._

- The newest Node.js LTS in the image (`node:24-alpine`); a bump is a
  change to the `Dockerfile`.
- Size the task's CPU and memory to the work, and the service's scaling
  bounds to the load the issue states; measure before raising them.
- Start-up: SDK clients created once, in `src/main.ts`; a small image
  (esbuild's bundle on Alpine, no `node_modules`), so a new task starts
  fast.
- Data access by key: `GetItem` and `Query` on keys and indexes; a `Scan` is
  never on a request path.
- By hand: measure a stated budget with a benchmark, and say what only a
  deployed service can show (start-up, scaling, latency in AWS).

## Cost optimization

_Deliver value at the lowest price: pay only for what you use, and know
where the money goes._

- A service runs all the time, so it costs by the hour whether busy or not:
  two small tasks, the load balancer and the NAT gateway are the floor.
  Scale-out has a ceiling; DynamoDB is on demand.
- `arm64` (Graviton) tasks: cheaper per vCPU-hour than x86.
- The DynamoDB gateway endpoint is free and keeps table traffic off the NAT
  gateway, which charges per gigabyte.
- Log retention limits what storage costs grow to (`infra/standard.test.ts`);
  access logs expire from their bucket after 30 days.
- Tags attribute every cost to its project, system and container.
- By hand: right-size the task and the scaling bounds; no second NAT
  gateway, interface endpoints or larger tasks unless the issue states the
  need.

## Sustainability

_Minimize the environmental impact of the workload._

- Fargate runs the tasks on shared infrastructure; scaling in to the
  minimum when idle keeps what runs to what the load needs.
- `arm64` (Graviton) uses less energy for the same work.
- Keep what is needed, no longer: log retention, and no data stored that
  the system's needs don't ask for.
- Efficient code: no polling, no work per request that can be done once at
  start-up, small images.
- No rule enforces these: review them.

## Acknowledging a finding

Fix the resource first. When the fix is wrong for this system, acknowledge
the one finding on the construct the finding's path names (or its nearest
ancestor you own): a finding on `Task/ExecutionRole/DefaultPolicy/Resource`
is acknowledged on the execution role, not on the task definition.

```ts
Validations.of(taskDefinition.obtainExecutionRole()).acknowledge({
  id: 'AwsSolutions-IAM5[Resource::*]',
  reason:
    'ecr:GetAuthorizationToken accepts only the resource "*"; every other action names its resource.',
});
```

The reason says why this resource needs it, true and specific; the reviewer
checks every one, and a vague reason ("not needed", "for now") is blocking.
