{{ if eq .Framework "aws-ec2-ts" }}# Well-Architected

A digest of the AWS Well-Architected Framework, for this repository: one
section per pillar, what the pillar asks of a service on EC2 instances in an
Auto Scaling group behind a load balancer, the CDK Nag rules (`AwsSolutions`
pack) that enforce it when `cdk synth` runs, and what you do by hand where no
rule does. Sources and the date they were read are in
`docs/style/SOURCES.md`. This file belongs to the Standard: a task never
changes it.

`make check` runs CDK Nag's `AwsSolutions` checks on every stack
(`infra/standard.ts`): a finding fails synth unless it is acknowledged, on
one construct, with a reason. `infra/standard.test.ts` checks what CDK Nag
doesn't: tags, wildcard actions, encryption, log retention, removal policies.

## Operational excellence

_Run and monitor the system, and improve it in small, reversible changes._

- Everything is code: the stacks in `infra/` are the only way resources are
  made, the instances' setup included (`bootCommands`). No change by hand in
  the console, and no logging in to an instance to fix it: replace it.
- Changes are small, frequent and checked: `make check` synthesizes and
  lints every change; nothing here deploys. A deployed change rolls through
  the Auto Scaling group one instance at a time.
- Observe: structured JSON logs with the request id, shipped by the
  CloudWatch agent to a log group; load balancer access logs in S3; rejected
  traffic in a VPC flow log; the group's metrics and the instances' detailed
  monitoring in CloudWatch.
  - `AwsSolutions-ELB2`: the load balancer writes access logs.
  - `AwsSolutions-VPC7`: the VPC has a flow log.
  - `AwsSolutions-EC28`: detailed monitoring on the instances.
  - `infra/standard.test.ts` checks that every log group has a retention.
- Tag everything so cost and ownership are traceable: every stack carries
  `c4factory:project`, `c4factory:system` and `c4factory:container`
  (`infra/standard.ts`, tested), and the Auto Scaling group passes them to
  its instances.
- By hand: alarms on 5xx responses, unhealthy hosts and latency, and a
  runbook, when the system's needs ask for them. Scaling notifications
  (`AwsSolutions-AS3`) are acknowledged until something subscribes to them.

## Security

_Protect data, systems and assets: identity, least privilege, encryption,
traceability, and protection at every layer._

- Least privilege: the instances have their own role, granted through CDK
  grants (`table.grant(role, 'dynamodb:GetItem')`) only the calls the server
  makes, and `s3:GetObject` on its own code.
  - `AwsSolutions-IAM4`: no AWS managed policies on a role.
  - `AwsSolutions-IAM5`: no wildcard actions or resources without an
    acknowledgement naming the one finding and why.
  - `infra/standard.test.ts`: no `*` or `service:*` action that allows.
- Protect the network: isolated subnets with no route to the internet;
  AWS services through VPC endpoints; the load balancer internal and open
  only to the VPC; the instances open only to the load balancer; no SSH key
  and no inbound rule for administration.
  - `AwsSolutions-EC23`: no security group open to `0.0.0.0/0` or `::/0`.
  - `AwsSolutions-EC27`: every security group says what it is for.
- Protect the instances: IMDSv2 required (a request forgery can't read the
  role's credentials); the server runs as its own user under systemd with
  `NoNewPrivileges` and a read-only system; Amazon Linux 2023's newest image
  at every replacement.
- Encrypt at rest and in transit.
  - `AwsSolutions-EC26`: EBS volumes are encrypted.
  - `AwsSolutions-S10`: the access log bucket refuses requests without TLS.
  - DynamoDB is always encrypted, and `infra/standard.test.ts` requires a
    KMS key (`SSESpecification`) on every table, encryption on every bucket,
    queue and topic.
  - Inside the VPC the load balancer speaks HTTP. Making it internet-facing
    is an issue's decision: it needs an HTTPS listener with a certificate,
    an authorizer in front of the routes, and an acknowledgement of
    `AwsSolutions-EC23` on its security group saying why the world may
    reach it.
- Protect data from the public: `AwsSolutions-S2` (bucket public access
  blocked).
- Secrets live in Secrets Manager, rotated (`AwsSolutions-SMG4`), read at
  start-up; never in the user data, the unit's environment, the template, a
  log or the repository.
- By hand: validate all input at the boundary; never log user data or
  tokens; never return internal errors to a client.

## Reliability

_Recover from failure, scale with demand, and keep working when a part
fails._

- Two Availability Zones: at least two instances, one per zone, and the load
  balancer in both.
- Replace what fails: the Auto Scaling group uses the load balancer's health
  check, so an instance that stops answering `GET /health` is replaced;
  systemd restarts a server that exits.
  - `AwsSolutions-AS2`: the group uses ELB health checks.
  - `AwsSolutions-AS1`: the group has a cooldown.
- Scale with demand: a target-tracking policy on CPU between the minimum and
  the maximum the stack sets.
- Change safely: a rolling update replaces one instance at a time and keeps
  one in service; a stopping instance gets 30 seconds to finish its requests
  (deregistration delay, `SIGTERM`). The update sends no resource signals,
  so it can't stop a release whose instances fail their health check:
  signals need `cfn-signal` and a CloudFormation endpoint, an issue's
  decision when the system's needs ask for it.
- Back up data and recover it: point-in-time recovery on every table, and
  data that outlives its stack.
  - `AwsSolutions-DDB3`: point-in-time recovery.
  - `AwsSolutions-RDS10`: deletion protection on databases; set it on tables
    too (`deletionProtection: true`).
  - `infra/standard.test.ts`: every stateful resource states its removal
    policy; data is `RETAIN`.
- Idempotency: a retried request changes nothing twice (whole-item puts,
  conditional writes, idempotency keys).
- Limits: a body size cap, keep-alive longer than the load balancer's idle
  timeout, and a request answered within it.

## Performance efficiency

_Use resources efficiently as demand changes and technology evolves._

- The newest Amazon Linux 2023 image and an LTS Node.js from its
  repositories.
- Size the instance to the work: measure before choosing a bigger type or a
  higher minimum.
- Start-up: SDK clients created once; the server bundled into one file by
  esbuild, so an instance starts without `npm install`.
- Data access by key: `GetItem` and `Query` on keys and indexes; a `Scan` is
  never on a request path.
- By hand: measure a stated budget with a benchmark, and say what only a
  deployed service can show (latency through the load balancer, scaling
  time).

## Cost optimization

_Deliver value at the lowest price: pay only for what you use, and know
where the money goes._

- Instances, the load balancer and the Logs endpoint cost by the hour
  whether or not there is work: keep the minimum at two and the type small
  unless the issue states the load that needs more.
- No NAT gateway: gateway endpoints for S3 and DynamoDB cost nothing, and
  one interface endpoint (Logs) costs less than a NAT gateway per zone.
- `arm64` (Graviton) instances: cheaper than x86 for the same work.
- On-demand DynamoDB, log retention, and access logs expired after 90 days
  limit what storage costs grow to.
- Tags attribute every cost to its project, system and container.
- By hand: Savings Plans or Reserved Instances are the account owner's
  choice, not the stack's.

## Sustainability

_Minimize the environmental impact of the workload._

- `arm64` (Graviton) uses less energy for the same work.
- Scale in when demand falls: the target-tracking policy removes instances
  down to the minimum.
- Keep what is needed, no longer: log retention, access logs expired, and no
  data stored that the system's needs don't ask for.
- Efficient code: no polling, no work per request that can be done once at
  start-up.
- No rule enforces these: review them.

{{ else if eq .Framework "aws-lambda-ts" "aws-queue-ts" }}{{ $queue := eq .Framework "aws-queue-ts" }}# Well-Architected

A digest of the AWS Well-Architected Framework and its Serverless Applications
Lens, for this repository: one section per pillar, what the pillar asks of a
serverless {{ if $queue }}queue consumer{{ else }}API{{ end }}, what each tier does about it, what `infra/operations.ts`
changes, the CDK Nag rules (`AwsSolutions` pack) and the Standard's checks
that enforce it, and what stays by hand, with the reason. Sources and the
date they were read are in `docs/style/SOURCES.md`. This file belongs to the
Standard: a task never changes it.

`make check` synthesizes every stack for both tiers, into
`cdk.out/production/` and `cdk.out/development/`, and runs CDK Nag's
`AwsSolutions` checks on both (`infra/standard.ts`): a finding fails synth
unless it is acknowledged, on one construct, with a reason.
`infra/standard.test.ts` checks, for both tiers, what CDK Nag doesn't: tags,
wildcard actions, encryption, log retention, removal policies, and every
promise below. `cfn-lint --include-checks I` lints both, with I3011 (a
stateful resource without a deletion policy) and I3013 (content that expires
without a set retention). The Serverless pack's rules are named where they
apply; it is not run, so they are review items.

## Tiers and the operations block

The tier is where the stacks run, and a deploy names it:
`cdk deploy -c tier=production`, or `development` when it names none. The
code never picks one, and nothing else is read at synth: a synth of a tier is
the same on every machine. A development stack's name ends in
`-development`, so a deploy that names no tier never changes a production
stack. `infra/standard.ts` (`TIER_RULES`) says what each tier means:

| Every stack                                     | `production`                        | `development`   |
| ----------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------- | --------------- |
| Data: tables, the backup vault and its key      | kept when the stack goes (`RETAIN`) | deleted with it |
| Deletion protection on tables, and on the stack | on                                  | off             |
| Point-in-time recovery on every table           | on                                  | off             |
| AWS Backup                                      | daily, for `retentionDays`, copied  | none            |
| X-Ray tracing                                   | on                                  | off             |
| Log retention                                   | one year                            | one month       |
| Alarms, their topic and a dashboard             | on                                  | on              |

What the Container promises in either tier is `infra/operations.ts`, which
the factory renders from its `spec.operations`; a change to the block is
written into the file, never the other way.

- `backups.retentionDays`: days AWS Backup keeps each daily backup in
  production; 0 keeps point-in-time recovery only.
- `backups.copyToRegion`: a second region every backup is copied to; empty
  for none.
- `recovery.rpo` and `recovery.rto`: the most data a restore may lose and
  the longest it may take, which `docs/runbook.md` is written to.
- `alerts.email`: the default of each stack's `AlertEmail` parameter, which
  a deploy sets per environment; empty tells nobody.

## Operational excellence

_Run and monitor the system, and improve it in small, reversible changes._

- Everything is code: the stacks in `infra/` are the only way resources are
  made. No change by hand in the console.
- Changes are small, frequent and checked: `make check` synthesizes and
  lints every change for both tiers; nothing here deploys.
- Observe (OPS08, REL06): structured JSON logs through Powertools' Logger,
  with the {{ if $queue }}message id{{ else }}request id{{ end }} and the Lambda context; metrics through Powertools'
  Metrics, written into the logs ({{ if $queue }}`ConsumedMessages`, `FailedMessages`{{ else }}`AnsweredRequests`, `FailedRequests`{{ end }}, the
  cold start); a log group per function{{ if $queue }}{{ else }} and an access log per API stage{{ end }};
  X-Ray traces in production.
{{- if not $queue }}
  - `AwsSolutions-APIG1`: the API has access logging.
{{- end }}
  - Serverless `CloudWatchLogGroupRetentionPeriod`{{ if not $queue }}, `APIGWStructuredLogging`{{ end }}:
    `infra/standard.test.ts` checks every log group keeps the tier's time.
- Alarms are code (OPS08-BP04, REL06-BP03), the same in both tiers:
  `cdk-monitoring-constructs` builds them on {{ if $queue }}the dead-letter queue's depth (any
  message), the oldest message's age (over 15 minutes), the function's
  errors, throttles and p99 duration (over 24 of its 30 seconds){{ else }}the API's 5xx, the function's
  errors, throttles and p99 duration (over 8 of its 10 seconds){{ end }}, and each
  table's read and write throttles and system errors, each firing on one bad
  five-minute period. Every alarm notifies the stack's one SNS topic,
  encrypted with its own key, and a dashboard per stack shows them
  (OPS08-BP05). `infra/standard.test.ts` checks that every function, table
  and {{ if $queue }}dead-letter queue{{ else }}API{{ end }} has its alarms and that every alarm notifies the topic: a new
  resource gets its alarms from the stack's `Alarms`
  (`alarms.monitoring.monitorDynamoTable(...)`).
- Nobody is told without asking: the `AlertEmail` parameter subscribes one
  address to the topic, and an empty one none. The topic's ARN is the
  stack's `AlarmTopicArn` output and {{ if $queue }}the queue's URL `EntryPoint` (and its ARN
  `QueueArn`), so a deploy target can subscribe its own channel and point
  producers at the queue{{ else }}its URL `EntryPoint`, so a deploy
  target can subscribe its own channel and find the API{{ end }}.
- A runbook: `docs/runbook.md` says what each alarm means and what to do
  first, how to restore the data and how to roll a deployment back. A
  change to what the stacks keep updates it, as a Standard change.
- Tag everything so cost and ownership are traceable: every stack carries
  `c4factory:project`, `c4factory:system` and `c4factory:container`
  (`infra/standard.ts`, tested).
- By hand, and why: whether the alarms fire for the right reasons, and
  which are missing (REL06-BP06), only a deployed stack's history shows;
  review it after every incident.

## Security

_Protect data, systems and assets: identity, least privilege, encryption,
traceability, and protection at every layer._

- Least privilege: each function has its own role, granted through CDK
  grants (`table.grant(fn, 'dynamodb:GetItem')`) only the calls its code
  makes.
  - `AwsSolutions-IAM4`: no AWS managed policies on a role, but AWS
    Backup's, whose `AWSBackupServiceRolePolicyForBackup` is acknowledged in
    `infra/backups.ts`.
  - `AwsSolutions-IAM5`: no wildcard actions or resources without an
    acknowledgement naming the one finding and why. X-Ray's write actions
    take no resource but `*`: `infra/standard.ts` acknowledges that, in
    production, on a policy where nothing else has it.
  - `infra/standard.test.ts`: no `*` or `service:*` action anywhere, but a
    key's default policy, which hands the key's use to the account's IAM.
{{- if $queue }}
- Authenticate every producer: the queue takes messages only from
  principals whose IAM policies allow `sqs:SendMessage` on `QueueArn`; the
  stack grants nobody but the function, which may only receive and delete.
{{- else }}
- Authenticate every request: every route has an authorizer (IAM, JWT or a
  Lambda authorizer).
  - `AwsSolutions-APIG4`: the API implements authorization.
  - `AwsSolutions-COG4`: a REST API method uses a Cognito authorizer.
{{- end }}
- Encrypt at rest and in transit.
  - `AwsSolutions-S10`, `AwsSolutions-SQS4`, `AwsSolutions-SNS3`: requests
    must use TLS; the alarm topic{{ if $queue }} and both queues (`enforceSSL`) refuse{{ else }} refuses{{ end }} any other.
  - `AwsSolutions-SQS2`, `AwsSolutions-EFS1`: encryption at rest; DynamoDB
    is always encrypted, and `infra/standard.test.ts` requires a KMS key
    (`SSESpecification`) on every table, encryption on every bucket, queue
    and topic.
  - Backups are encrypted with the vault's own key (REL09-BP02); the alarm
    topic with its own, which CloudWatch may use to publish.
  - `AwsSolutions-KMS5`: customer-managed keys rotate.
- Protect data from the public: `AwsSolutions-S2` (bucket public access
  blocked), `AwsSolutions-S5`, `AwsSolutions-EVB1` (no open event bus
  policy). `AwsSolutions-APIG3` and `AwsSolutions-CFR2` warn when a REST API
  or a distribution has no WAF.
- Secrets live in Secrets Manager, rotated (`AwsSolutions-SMG4`), read at
  start-up; never in an environment variable, the template, a log or the
  repository.
- The account's own guards (GuardDuty, Security Hub, CloudTrail, AWS Config)
  are its owner's, not a stack's: `docs/runbook.md` lists them.
- By hand: validate all input at the boundary{{ if $queue }}, a message's body included,
  whoever sent it{{ end }}; never log user data or
  tokens; never return internal errors to a client.

## Reliability

_Recover from failure, scale with demand, and keep working when a part
fails._

- Managed, multi-AZ services: Lambda, {{ if $queue }}SQS{{ else }}API Gateway{{ end }} and DynamoDB spread over
  Availability Zones on their own.
- Recovery objectives (REL13-BP01): `recovery.rpo` and `recovery.rto` say
  what a restore in production may lose and how long it may take;
  `docs/runbook.md` is written to them.
- Back up data (REL09), in production:
  - Point-in-time recovery on every table: any second of the last 35 days,
    up to about five minutes before now, restored to a new table. It meets
    an RPO of minutes.
  - An AWS Backup plan (REL09-BP03): a daily backup at 05:00 UTC of every
    resource carrying the Container's tag, so a new table is in it, kept
    `retentionDays` in a vault with its own key (REL09-BP02); with
    `copyToRegion`, each copied to a vault in that region (the
    `<name>-backup-copy` stack), so a lost region loses no more than a day
    (REL13-BP02, backup and restore).
  - No vault lock: in compliance mode it can never be undone, which is the
    account owner's decision.
  - Development backs up nothing: its data goes with its stack.
- Nothing of value is deleted by accident, in production: tables are
  `RETAIN` with deletion protection, and the stack has termination
  protection. A change that replaces a table (a new key) keeps the old one
  but leaves it outside the stack, empty of the new one's data: review
  every change to a table's key as a migration.
  - `AwsSolutions-DDB3`: point-in-time recovery; `infra/standard.ts`
    acknowledges it in development only.
  - `infra/standard.test.ts`: per tier, the removal policies, the
    protections, point-in-time recovery, the backup plan, its vault and its
    copy.
  - cfn-lint I3011: every stateful resource has a deletion policy.
- Restore by a written procedure (REL09-BP04, REL13-BP05):
  `docs/runbook.md` restores the items to a point in time, from the vault,
  from the copy, and a deleted table, and rolls a deployment back.
- Handle failures in asynchronous work: a queue or event source has a
  dead-letter queue.
  - `AwsSolutions-SQS3`: a queue has a DLQ or is one.
{{- if $queue }}
  - The queue's redrive policy moves a message to the dead-letter queue
    after five deliveries (Serverless `SQSRedrivePolicy`), where it waits
    14 days and its first message fires an alarm; `docs/runbook.md` sends
    it back once the cause is fixed. `infra/standard.test.ts` checks every
    dead-letter queue has that alarm.
  - Partial batch responses: the event source mapping reports
    `ReportBatchItemFailures`, and Powertools' batch processing answers the
    messages that failed, so one bad message is delivered again alone and
    never holds back its batch. `infra/standard.test.ts` checks every SQS
    event source sets it. Serverless `LambdaEventSourceMappingDestination`
    asks for an on-failure destination, which SQS sources don't take: their
    dead-letter queue is the queue's.
  - The queue's visibility timeout is six times the function's timeout, so
    a message is not delivered again while a slow batch still holds it.
{{- else }}
  - Serverless `LambdaDLQ`, `LambdaAsyncFailureDestination`,
    `SQSRedrivePolicy`, `EventBusDLQ`: by hand, for asynchronous invocations;
    an API Gateway call is synchronous, and its client retries.
{{- end }}
{{- if $queue }}
- Idempotency: SQS delivers a message at least once, so a message
  delivered again changes nothing twice. Powertools' Idempotency keys each
  message by its `messageId` and keeps the record for an hour in the
  stack's idempotency table; a failed message keeps none, so its next
  delivery runs again. Storing an item puts the whole item, so a producer
  that sends it twice leaves the same item.
- Limits: a timeout on every function, and the event source's maximum
  concurrency (10) leaves the account's concurrency for its other
  functions; the queue holds what waits. Serverless
  `LambdaFunctionDefaultTimeout`: by hand.
{{- else }}
- Idempotency: a retried request changes nothing twice. A `PUT` replaces
  the whole item, and one sent again with the same `Idempotency-Key` header
  gets the first answer back: Powertools' Idempotency keeps it for an hour
  in the stack's idempotency table. A refusal or a failure keeps nothing,
  so a corrected retry runs again; the key sent with another path or body
  is refused (422), and a retry while the first still runs too (409).
- Limits: timeouts on every function, below the caller's (API Gateway: 30
  seconds); throttling on every API stage, so a burst can't exhaust the
  account's concurrency. Serverless `LambdaFunctionDefaultTimeout`,
  `APIGWDefaultThrottling`: by hand.
{{- end }}
- By hand, and why: a restore drill (REL09-BP04, REL13-BP03), at least a
  quarter apart, and a game day once a year; only an account shows that a
  restore works and how long it takes, so the RTO is a promise until a drill
  measures it. `docs/runbook.md` says how.

